WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
- What I always say

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.791
is I'd like to hire a neurologist

00:00:01.791 --> 00:00:04.720
to study people watching animation.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.882
I'd love to find out what
parts of the brain light up.

00:00:06.882 --> 00:00:10.049
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:12.094 --> 00:00:14.610
- We're so glad, first of all,

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:15.846
to be able to make the show.

00:00:15.846 --> 00:00:20.634
And our first goal was
to not ruin the TV show.

00:00:20.634 --> 00:00:23.614
We wanted to make a
movie only if we could do

00:00:23.614 --> 00:00:27.630
a good job on both
things at the same time.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.930
Then the next goal was to make something

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:31.665
that could truly be a movie.

00:00:31.665 --> 00:00:33.950
We love making a TV show,

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.551
we feel very lucky to be able to do that.

00:00:36.551 --> 00:00:39.290
It's a unique thing to make a TV show

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:40.770
that's lasted this long.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.350
You really know the fans really have

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.830
a relationship with those characters.

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:46.590
That's a long relationship, some of

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.450
those fans have been
watching the whole time.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:51.272
However, all of us that work
on the show love movies.

00:00:51.272 --> 00:00:55.215
And it was very exciting
to be able to make one.

00:00:55.215 --> 00:00:58.993
I have never had the pleasure
and I may never again.

00:00:58.993 --> 00:01:01.090
The fact that it's an adaptation

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:02.560
of an existing television show had

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.255
to be both our everyday reality

00:01:05.255 --> 00:01:07.630
and also something that we almost tried

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.110
to ignore at the same time.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:11.480
We had to make it a
movie that slots right in

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.402
to the TV show and leaves
no fans disappointed

00:01:14.402 --> 00:01:17.720
and hopefully drop some
right back where they were.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.310
They can pick up and watch season 12 after

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.010
they finished the movie but we also had

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:24.920
to make a movie that could
live without the TV show.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.530
If there's somebody out
there who hasn't seen

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.730
the show and walks into the theater,

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:31.032
I want them to have the feeling like,

00:01:31.032 --> 00:01:33.203
"Holy crap, what a good movie!"

00:01:33.203 --> 00:01:36.120
And they may or may not wanna go watch

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.368
the TV show after that and
that is absolutely fine.

00:01:38.368 --> 00:01:41.261
We wanted to service that person too.

00:01:41.261 --> 00:01:44.510
(upbeat music)

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:45.380
- One of the pitfalls

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:47.995
that we thought would be there, temptation

00:01:47.995 --> 00:01:50.094
when you wanna go bigger with the story,

00:01:50.094 --> 00:01:52.925
one urge is to make your world bigger.

00:01:52.925 --> 00:01:55.961
And we didn't want to get tempted by that.

00:01:55.961 --> 00:01:58.210
We made it a little bigger here and there.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.060
We pushed out at the seams a little bit,

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.550
but what we ended up trying

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:03.510
to do instead was make it deeper.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.380
We wanted to take the same
locations you've already seen

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:10.235
and tell you something you
didn't know about that spot.

00:02:10.235 --> 00:02:13.060
The same people you already knew

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.320
and tell you something you
didn't know about them.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:17.160
We knew it, we wanted to it to be

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.070
a mystery because we love that genre.

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.519
And we knew it would give us the stakes,

00:02:21.519 --> 00:02:25.560
the higher stakes, we knew
we wanted to have music

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.090
because we knew it would
help us with spectacle.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:31.820
And we knew that we wanted to have

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:35.133
these action set pieces
because of the same thing.

00:02:35.133 --> 00:02:37.931
It would fill the screen and make

00:02:37.931 --> 00:02:41.290
the audience feel like they got something

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.290
for their money that was worth leaving

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:44.660
the house to come see this thing.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:46.523
And I think the part that
we worked the hardest on,

00:02:46.523 --> 00:02:49.730
was figuring out something that was there

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:52.822
in the show all along, but
we hadn't explored yet.

00:02:52.822 --> 00:02:55.310
You are really having to dig

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.348
and root around like
some truffle pig, trying

00:02:57.348 --> 00:02:59.804
to find that story that somehow,

00:02:59.804 --> 00:03:02.500
you haven't found up until that point.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:03.720
That was the real work,

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.470
cause can we make this story true

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.812
to the show, make it
true to the characters,

00:03:07.812 --> 00:03:11.033
but it's emotional stuff that
we haven't explored before.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.436
We love our crew, we have a great group

00:03:16.436 --> 00:03:20.160
in many, many, many,
many, many departments.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.220
We are talking about people who've been

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:23.430
there since the very beginning.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:24.510
Bernard is one of those people.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:28.568
He boarded on season one from
Australia, Bernard DeRman

00:03:28.568 --> 00:03:33.104
and then we enticed him
to come join the crew.

00:03:33.104 --> 00:03:35.900
And then we further enticed
him to become a director.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.693
So he was the perfect
person to direct this movie.

00:03:39.693 --> 00:03:43.229
We then added very carefully, mostly

00:03:43.229 --> 00:03:46.800
we took our folks from the series and gave

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.662
them roles on the film, but
we did add a few people.

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:52.050
And I think it's worth highlighting

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:53.950
our art director, Ruben Hickman,

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:56.103
because he wasn't on the series with us.

00:03:56.103 --> 00:03:58.739
He came from a movie background.

00:03:58.739 --> 00:04:01.415
Ultimately we feel, even though none

00:04:01.415 --> 00:04:03.448
of us had made a movie before, we all felt

00:04:03.448 --> 00:04:05.750
at least we're experts on Bob's.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:09.610
But what Ruben did that
kind of made us all sit up

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.250
a little higher in our
seats that every meeting

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.866
was he brought light in a way to

00:04:14.866 --> 00:04:19.090
the backgrounds, especially but really to

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:22.340
the whole movie it just
suddenly was infused

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.120
with this kind of emotion

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.600
that was waiting to be drawn out.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.620
But it just hadn't quite been lit yet.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:31.830
What I always like to say it was like

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:33.987
we got Edward Hopper to come in

00:04:33.987 --> 00:04:36.435
and paint the backgrounds of Bob's.

00:04:36.435 --> 00:04:39.810
(gentle music)

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.947
I love animation, I've only ever worked

00:04:41.947 --> 00:04:44.370
in animation before
that, I was bartending.

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.808
I've never aspired to work in live action.

00:04:46.808 --> 00:04:49.110
I love live action and I watch a lot of it

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.230
but I don't need to tell
stories in that medium.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:55.360
I like this medium and movies are special.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:56.820
They really are, TV's wonderful.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:59.800
And I love making a TV
show but movies have

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.190
this possibility of being elevated.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:04.240
First of all, you're gonna go sit

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:05.100
with a bunch of other people.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.020
You're gonna sit in
the dark and experience

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.499
that thing that can only happen

00:05:09.499 --> 00:05:11.710
in a room with other people.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:13.320
And that's just part of being human.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:18.320
And then animation is weird, I think

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:20.310
it enters the brain in a different way.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:22.395
I think 2D animation in particular is

00:05:22.395 --> 00:05:25.526
a really beautiful medium
that has a lot going on.

00:05:25.526 --> 00:05:27.320
What I always say is I'd like to hire

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:30.640
a neurologist to study
people watching animation.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.100
I'd love to find out what parts of

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.420
the brain light up when you see Bob

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:37.340
and his family versus if we showed you

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:39.160
the same movie in live action.

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.810
I'm convinced there's some we're sneaking

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.461
in the back door of
people's minds and lighting

00:05:44.461 --> 00:05:47.594
up different parts of the consciousness

00:05:47.594 --> 00:05:51.820
and probably unconsciousness
because animation's

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:54.230
so strange, it's like a
strange little magic trick

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:56.646
that we we try to pull off every week.

00:05:56.646 --> 00:05:59.813
(gentle fading music)

